  [FAIL] cb-7 the verdict must not flip between reasonable membership and deprojection choices   (sigma from framework spans 2.8 to 4.7)
  [FAIL] cb-8 THE DECIDING CHECK: for K5 to be a candidate second law the framework's predicted external-field slope must match the measured one within 3 sigma.  Written so it can go either way.   (canonical 4.54 sigma, alt 4.34 sigma; LambdaCDM (slope 0) sits at 0.11 sigma)

================================================================================================================
  VERDICT ON CANDIDATE K5
================================================================================================================
  The test IS runnable on data already in the repository -- 314 inclined, S/N > 6.5 ALFALFA
  spirals inside 5 R_500 of 104 PSZ2 clusters, spanning g_e/a_0 = 0.05 to 0.4.
  The proposing agent recorded it as needing a fetch; it did not.

  A BUG OF MY OWN, FOUND AND FIXED IN THE MAKING, recorded rather than quietly corrected: the first
  run fed the cluster's TRUE field g_e = G M(<r)/r^2 straight into nu, where nu's argument is the
  NEWTONIAN field.  In a deep-MOND cluster outskirt those differ by an order of magnitude
  (here g_e/a_0 = 0.090 against g_Ne/a_0 = 0.0074 at the median), and the error inflated the predicted
  deficit about threefold: the uncorrected run reported 5.1 sigma on the slope and 12.5 sigma on the
  zero point.  Corrected, both shrink.  Same species as bug pattern 1 -- a quantity used in a slot
  that belongs to a different one.

  MEASURED   d[log V - (1/4)log(G M_b a_0)] / d log(g_e/a_0) = +0.0033 +/- 0.0304
  FRAMEWORK  -0.1348 (canonical) / -0.1290 (alt)
  LambdaCDM  0.0000 exactly (strong equivalence principle)
  -> 4.5 sigma from the framework (canonical), 4.3 sigma (alt); 0.1 sigma from LambdaCDM.

  The ZERO-POINT version of the same test (check cb-5) points the same way but far more weakly:
  members sit -0.0119 +/- 0.0092 dex from the field BTFR at matched M_b and M_HI, against a
  predicted -0.0336 -- 2.4 sigma from the framework and 1.3 from LambdaCDM.  The SLOPE
  carries the weight, not the offset, because the offset averages over a range of external fields
  most of which are weak.

  NOT a second Kepler-grade law.  It fails criterion (2) in the only way that matters: the predicted
  coefficient is not the measured one.  The candidate is a LIABILITY for the framework, not a law --
  and it is the same liability the hunt already carries from item 9 (Coma ultra-diffuse galaxies
  +1.195 dex above the external-field prediction), item 63 (the environment sign at 2 sigma), and
  item 48/69 (binary galaxies 26 sigma above the external-field branch).  This is a fourth
  independent measurement of the same thing: where the external-field effect should bite, it does
  not.  Reported as such.

  HONEST LIMITS OF THIS RUN, stated before any reading is taken from it:
   * W50 is a global line width, not a resolved rotation curve; hunt item 124 measured a +0.25 dex
     per dex width-selection bias across three decades of mass, and any of it that correlates with
     cluster membership propagates straight into this slope.
   * R_HI comes from the Wang+2016 scaling relation, not from a map.  It sets where the external
     field is compared with the internal one, so its 0.06 dex scatter is a floor on the prediction.
   * the projected radius is a LOWER bound on the 3-D radius, so g_e is an UPPER bound -- the
     direction that favours a detection.  The deprojected variant is in the robustness table.
   * the external field is combined with the internal one as a SCALAR SUM, the maximal-EFE
     orientation; the repository's own ledger records that this prescription over-predicts DF2 by
     2x against a careful published calculation, so the predicted deficit here is an upper bound.
   * ram pressure and starvation remove HI in clusters in EITHER theory.  M_HI is measured, so it is
     in M_b, but a galaxy stripped of its outer HI has a SMALLER R_HI than the relation gives and
     its W50 samples a different part of the curve.  This is the confound that would have to be
     controlled with resolved data before any positive result here could be believed.

RESULT: 9 checks, 2 FAIL -> ['cb-7 the verdict must not flip between reasonable membership and deprojection choices', "cb-8 THE DECIDING CHECK: for K5 to be a candidate second law the framework's predicted external-field slope must match the measured one within 3 sigma.  Written so it can go either way."]   rc=1
